 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §contiguous-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs in order to `t1…tn` and resets `next` to n+1, so keys are always
+  contiguous (`t1,t2,t3` minus `t2` → `t1,t2`).
+- Supersedes: §stable-tab-keys, overridden by the maintainer on 2026-10-22.
+- Known cost: the key is the React key and the URL state. Closing a tab changes the key of every tab to its right, so
+  React remounts them and they lose scroll position and unsaved form input (the 2026-08-29 incident behavior).
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "open after close continues the contiguous sequence")
+
+### §stable-tab-keys — superseded 2026-10-22 by §contiguous-tab-keys
+- Since: 2026-09-02, superseded 2026-10-22
+- Was: a key was assigned once from `next` and never reused or shifted; closing a tab left a gap (`t1,t3`).
+- Was forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close.
+- Original reason: shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one and drops its scroll position and
+  unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29).
